    {
      "ordinal": 4,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "1.2",
      "title": "Types of tabards",
      "body": "● Sleeveless tabard : Tabards without sleeves. Simple design, common in the 13th century and 14th century. Tend to be rare in the 15th century.\n● Short-sleeves tabard : Tabards with short sleeves that can go up to the elbow.\nCommon design in the 14th century. Tend to be rare in the 15th century.\n● Long-sleeves tabard : Tabards with long sleeves that cover the whole arms, from shoulder to hand. Common design in the 14th and the 15th century.\n● Eastern tabard : We do not have many historical references for eastern tabard. It is allowed to use the same tabard as European armors. Eastern stylization of tabard are displayed in the document “Eastern Armors”.\n● Exception for full plates armors : Wearing full plates armor makes the use of tabard not practical. Therefore, it is allowed to wear only a scarf. Scarf must follow the general requirements (1.3).\n\nExample of scarf:\n\nPicture from The Medieval Things ©",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
      "stable_key": "tabards:1.2",
