      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "4.1",
      "title": "Historical sources and datation",
      "body": "● Long-sleeves tabard combine the role of exposing your heraldry and protecting your armor. The sleeves often cover the whole arm, up to the wrist, but can be shorter (up to mid-forearm). Many different designs can be documented by historical sources.\n● They are approved for 14th century, Transitional and 15th century armors.\n\nA B C D\n\nA: Guiron le Courtois - 1380 - France B: Knebel von Katzenelnbogen - 1401 - Germany C: Bible historiale, BL Royal 15 D III - 1420 - France D: Le Livre et le vraye hystoire du bon roy Alixandre - 1420 - France",
      "page_from": 10,
      "page_to": 10,
